    {
      "id": "export-template-view",
      "type": "view",
      "label": "View",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 460,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "export",
          "nodeId": "export-template-view"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;height:100%;padding:28px;background:#ffffff;font-family:system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow:auto'><h1 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 24px/1.2 system-ui'>Bridge: the export half</h1><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>This graph exposes <code>widget.ready</code> (declared by the Event Source Method below) to <a href='github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/bridge-template/import/root.node'>bridge-template/import</a>, by name, via <code>data.bridge.exposure.events.allowlist</code>. The pairing is real: each side's Bridge node carries the other graph's actual ref, the same way qa/custom-event-bridge-smoke's source/receiver pair does -- this template is built from that exact precedent.</p><h2 style='margin:20px 0 8px;font:700 16px/1.3 system-ui'>What loads automatically, and what doesn't</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Opening either side of this pair makes <code>useBackgroundBridgeInstances.js</code> follow the Bridge ref to the other side, transitively, so the Event can actually route -- you never have to open both graphs by hand for the subscription to work. What you <em>see</em> happen depends on entitlement, not on anything you choose per-graph: on <strong>free tier</strong> the other side loads silently into the hook's own background map -- no tile, no layout, invisible, existing purely so event dispatch can still find it. On <strong>premium</strong> (<code>hasWorkspaceAccess()</code>), the exact same discovery instead calls <code>workspace.open()</code> and the other side appears as a real, visible workspace tile -- the paid \"see the topography\" feature, not a different bridging mechanism.</p><h2 style='margin:20px 0 8px;font:700 16px/1.3 system-ui'>Why that open() call passes <code>{ background: true }</code></h2><p style='margin:0;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Earlier this session, auto-loaded bridges were silently stealing focus: whichever one happened to finish resolving last became the active graph, overriding wherever the person had actually navigated to by hand. The fix threaded a <code>background</code> option through <code>workspace.open()</code> -- when true, the new instance still becomes a real, visible tile, it just skips every <code>focusLoadedInstance</code>/<code>setAddress</code> call that would otherwise steal the active selection. This is exactly the call this template's own auto-load goes through, so if that option ever looks like unused-looking dead code during a cleanup pass, it isn't -- removing it reintroduces the focus-stealing bug this pair would otherwise reproduce every time someone opens it on premium.</p></div>"
        }
      }
    },
